{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f27e39d99b57bc5b28", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Structure of Atom - Test 3", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 37, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f4fe"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f488", "title": "Chemistry Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f41e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f27"], "question": {"text": "<p>The difference between the radii of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>rd</mi></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>4</mn><mtext>th&#160;</mtext></msup></math> orbits of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#916;R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. The difference between the radii of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>rd</mi></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>4</mn><mtext>th&#160;</mtext></msup></math> orbits of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>He</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math> Ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>&#916;</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "e135f4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f41f"}, {"id": "0c7810", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f420"}, {"id": "bf3863", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f421"}, {"id": "c8fe64", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f422"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f423", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b78"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b42"], "question": {"text": "The candela is the luminous intensity, in a given direction, of a source that emits monochromatic radiation of frequency ' \\(\\mathrm{A}\\) ' \\(\\times 10^{12}\\) hertz and that has a radiant intensity in that direction of \\(\\frac{1}{'\\mathrm{~B}^{\\prime}}\\) watt per steradian.\n'A' and 'B' are respectively"}, "options": [{"id": "0a5db5", "text": "540 and 683", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f424"}, {"id": "7a0908", "text": "450 and 683", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f425"}, {"id": "44b8ba", "text": "450 and \\(\\frac{1}{683}\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f426"}, {"id": "f5b8d3", "text": "540 and \\(\\frac{1}{683}\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f427"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f428", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9268"], "question": {"text": "<p>Consider the following statements :</p><p>(A) The principal quantum number &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>&#39; is a positive integer with values of &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>&#39;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo></math></p><p>(B) The azimuthal quantum number &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math>&#39; for a given &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>&#39; (principal quantum number) can have values as &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math>&#39; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math> has&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;values.</p><p>(C) Magnetic orbital quantum number '$\\mathrm{m}$' for a particular '$\\mathrm{l}$' (azimuthal quantum number) has $(2\\mathrm{l}+1)$ values.</p><p>(D) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#177;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> are the two possible orientations of electron spin.</p><p>(E) For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math>, there will be a total of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;orbital</p><p>Which of the above statements are correct?</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "8e1ebc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f429"}, {"id": "098938", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mfenced><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f42a"}, {"id": "f7faa8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f42b"}, {"id": "4d7658", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f42c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f42d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87da"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following is/are not correct with respect to energy of atomic orbitals of hydrogen atom?\n(A)$1 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 2 \\mathrm{p} \\lt 3 \\mathrm{~d} \\lt 4 \\mathrm{~s}$\n(B) $1 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 2 \\mathrm{~s}=2 \\mathrm{p} \\lt 3 \\mathrm{~s}=3 \\mathrm{p}$\n(C) $1 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 2 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 2 \\mathrm{p} \\lt 3 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 3 \\mathrm{p}$\n(D) $1 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 2 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 4 \\mathrm{~s} \\lt 3 \\mathrm{~d}$\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "de61a4", "text": "(A) and (C) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f42e"}, {"id": "bea0e0", "text": "(B) and (D) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f42f"}, {"id": "02f850", "text": "(C) and (D) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f430"}, {"id": "648a8e", "text": "(A) and (B) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f431"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f432", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477c"], "concepts": ["61f95d0ff4072e13fa370897"], "question": {"text": "Which transition in the hydrogen spectrum would have the same wavelength as the Balmer type transition from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>He</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>spectrum"}, "options": [{"id": "6bff45", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f433"}, {"id": "1b47a1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f434"}, {"id": "ba8935", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f435"}, {"id": "99cb8e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f436"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f437", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eee"], "question": {"text": "The graph between <math><msup><mrow><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#968;</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> and <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math> (radical distance) is shown below. This represents:<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F3566efd8-a2cd-4f09-9b4b-a17a518848c2-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 241px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "567516", "text": "<math><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> orbital", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f438"}, {"id": "a509d3", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> orbital", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f439"}, {"id": "c5f590", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> orbital", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f43a"}, {"id": "e286e2", "text": "<math><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi></math> orbital", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f43b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f43c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["6162fb756ed13040ec84f23b"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements :<br />Statement I : According to Bohr&#39;s model of an atom, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with decrease in positive charges on the nucleus as there is no strong hold on the electron by the nucleus.<br />Statement II : According to Bohr&#39;s model of an atom, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with decrease in principle quantum number.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "ce01fd", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f43d"}, {"id": "e60798", "text": "Statement I is true but Statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f43e"}, {"id": "55649d", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f43f"}, {"id": "b00d06", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f440"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f441", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477c"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f9943"], "question": {"text": "The region in the electromagnetic&nbsp;spectrum where the Balmer series lines appear is:"}, "options": [{"id": "d6e4c2", "text": "Visible", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f442"}, {"id": "5c6420", "text": "Microwave", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f443"}, {"id": "ac7d7a", "text": "Infrared", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f444"}, {"id": "377047", "text": "Ultraviolet", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f445"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f446", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b78"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5671"], "question": {"text": "If the radius of the first orbit of hydrogen atom is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn mathvariant=\"italic\">0</mn></msub></math>, then de Broglie&rsquo;s wavelength of electron in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>rd</mi></msup></math> orbit is"}, "options": [{"id": "c0d136", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f447"}, {"id": "b68226", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f448"}, {"id": "e67fdb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f449"}, {"id": "f9908e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f44a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f44b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["61f95d47f4072e13fa370bdc"], "question": {"text": "The extra stability of half-filled subshell is due to<br />(A) Symmetrical distribution of electrons<br />(B) Smaller coulombic repulsion energy<br />(C) The presence of electrons with the same spin in non-degenerate orbitals<br />(D) Larger exchange energy<br />(E) Relatively smaller shielding of electrons by one another<br />Identify the correct statements"}, "options": [{"id": "07cc1d", "text": "(B), (D) and (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f44c"}, {"id": "0046bb", "text": "$(\\mathrm{A}),(\\mathrm{B}),(\\mathrm{D})$ and (E) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f44d"}, {"id": "153017", "text": "(B), (C) and (D) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f44e"}, {"id": "943abe", "text": "(A), (B) and (D) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f44f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f450", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["6162fb796ed13040ec84f523"], "question": {"text": "The electrons are more likely to be found:<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F7f1df81b-88d7-499f-a053-737f39b0fc71-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 260px; height: 251px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "d827a9", "text": "Only in the region a", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f451"}, {"id": "83d360", "text": "In the region a and b", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f452"}, {"id": "7e0adf", "text": "In the region a and c", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f453"}, {"id": "2e5e2c", "text": "Only in the region c", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f454"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f455", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["6162fb756ed13040ec84f247"], "question": {"text": "Identify the incorrect statement from the following."}, "options": [{"id": "e3f49d", "text": "A circular path around the nucleus in which an electron moves is proposed as Bohr&#39;s orbit.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f456"}, {"id": "ff4a2b", "text": "An orbital is the one electron wave function", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f457"}, {"id": "c5db64", "text": "The existence of Bohr&#39;s orbits is supported by hydrogen spectrum.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f458"}, {"id": "f5b65f", "text": "Atomic orbital is characterised by the quantum numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math>&nbsp;only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f459"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f45a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477c"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52d5"], "question": {"text": "For the Balmer series, in the spectrum of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> atom<em>,</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi><mo>-</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mfenced open=\"{\" close=\"}\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo></math> the correct statements among <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> to <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> are,<br /><math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> As wavelength decreases, the lines in the series converge.<br /><math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> The integer <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> is equal to <math><mn>2</mn></math>/<br /><math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> The lines of the longest wavelength correspond&nbsp;to <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>.<br /><math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> The ionization energy of hydrogen can be calculated from the wave number of these lines."}, "options": [{"id": "a6a51c", "text": "<math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f45b"}, {"id": "197bce", "text": "<math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f45c"}, {"id": "2c998f", "text": "<math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f45d"}, {"id": "c0ccae", "text": "<math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi></mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f45e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f45f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87da"], "question": {"text": "Compare the energies of following sets of quantum numbers for multielectron system.\n(A) \\(\\mathrm{n}=4,1=1\\)\n(B) \\(\\mathrm{n}=4, \\mathrm{l}=2\\)\n(C) \\(\\mathrm{n}=3,1=1\\)\n(D) \\(\\mathrm{n}=3,1=2\\)\n(E) \\(\\mathrm{n}=4,1=0\\)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "0682a1", "text": "\\(\\text { (B) } > \\text { (A) } > \\text { (C) } > \\text { (E) } > \\text { (D) }\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f460"}, {"id": "cfdd4f", "text": "\\(\\text { (E) }  < (\\text { C }) < \\text { (D) } < (\\text { A }) < (\\text { B })\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f461"}, {"id": "f0ac86", "text": "\\(\\text { (E) } > (\\text { C }) > (\\text { A }) > (\\text { D }) > (B)\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f462"}, {"id": "f6a8ba", "text": "\\(\\text { (C) }  < \\text { (E) } < \\text { (D) } < \\text { (A) } < \\text { (B) }\\)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f463"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f464", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b78"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b42"], "question": {"text": "<p>The minimum energy that must be possessed by photons in order to produce the photoelectric effect with platinum metal is:</p><p>[Given: The threshold frequency of platinum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Js</mi></math>]</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "0379a3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f465"}, {"id": "db1e25", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>76</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f466"}, {"id": "88f1e7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f467"}, {"id": "464b8d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f468"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f469", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4746"], "question": {"text": "<p>The spin only magnetic moments (in BM) for free <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ti</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Sc</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> ions respectively are</p><p>(At.No. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Sc</mi></math> : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Ti</mi><mo>:</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>:</mo><mn>23</mn></math>&nbsp;)</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "7ee24d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>73</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f46a"}, {"id": "f7e472", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>73</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6dc677dc36a891f46b"}, {"id": "51cc50", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>73</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f